WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.680
A star near our galaxy's central black hole is still forming dust and water, despite extreme radiation. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 159. NASA's James Webb Space Telescope captured mid-infrared signatures of oxygen-rich silicate dust and water in the envelope around IRS-3. The star lies just over half a light year from the supermassive black hole, and is nearing the end of its life. Powerful stellar winds from similar cool,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:59.560
luminous stars normally shed gas that condenses into dust. The new data show this process continues even in the harsh environment close to Sagittarius A star. Similar huge stars shed dust-producing gas with their powerful stellar winds, yet the observations confirm dust formation persists near the black hole. Follow-up observations will track how much material survives to seed future star formation. Webb's detection of water and oxygen-rich dust around

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:25.060
IRS-3 shows that even stars within half a light year of Sagittarius A star can still build the raw materials for future planets and stars. The mid-infrared spectra reveal silicate features and water-ice absorption that persists despite intense radiation and stellar winds from the Galactic Center. This survival suggests dust production channels remain viable in environments once thought to hostile.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:46.420
The next step is to measure how much of this material escapes the black hole's gravitational influence and joins the Galactic Reservoir. Picture a single teaspoon of the dust around IRS-3 scaled up to the volume of a city block. That tiny mass, spread across half a light year, is enough to see thousands of future solar systems.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:58.440
The mechanism begins when the aging star's powerful winds compress and cool its outer layers, allowing silicon and oxygen atoms to condense into solid grains before the material can be torn apart.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:07.520
Temperatures in these shielded pockets drop below 1000 Kelvin, even while the surrounding radiation field exceeds anything we experience on Earth.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:17.520
The grains then lock water molecules onto their surfaces, creating icy mantles that further protect the silicates, yet the grains must thread a narrow path.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:31.940
Too close to the black hole and tidal forces shred them, too far and they never form at all. Radiation pressure from the central engine can also accelerate the grains outward, but only if they survive the initial condensation phase.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:39.740
We still cannot explain why some envelopes retain measurable water, while others at similar distances appear stripped bare.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:49.080
The precise balance between wind shielding and external irradiation remains an open puzzle that future multi-wavelength monitoring may resolve.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:03:02.200
Now the European Space Agency will air live coverage of today's total solar eclipse, beginning at 1930 Central European Summertime, from the Observatorio Astrophesico de Habalambre in Spain.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:11.000
The path crosses Iceland in the afternoon local time, and reaches Spain later, with totality visible across parts of the northern hemisphere.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:21.600
NASA will also stream views starting at 1.15 pm eastern time. The broadcast includes real-time imagery and expert commentary for viewers outside the path.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:31.900
Final rehearsal is underway at the Spanish Observatory ahead of the live transmission. Fingers crossed for clear skies wherever observers sit along the eclipse path.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:42.340
On a different note, a Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg at 0446 UTC, and successfully released 24 Starlink satellites into orbit.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:52.880
The mission marks another step in expanding the constellation's coverage at mid-latitudes. Telemetry confirmed all spacecraft separated cleanly and began commissioning.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:04:00.840
The launch adds to the ongoing build-out of the Broadband Network. Ground teams verified deployment through post-separation signals.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:10.220
Shifting focus, the prototype satellite launched in July 2025, had been steadily losing altitude until recent maneuvers reversed the decay.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:23.360
Amazon engineers executed a series of burns that raised the orbit and resumed raising operations. The recovery demonstrates the satellite's propulsion system can still function after extended low orbit exposure.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:31.020
Observers had ridden the spacecraft off before the successful correction. Orbit-raising efforts are now back on track.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:43.220
Next, the H3 upper stage delivered the navigation satellite into a 390 by 35,374 km orbit at 22.9 degrees inclination.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:48.540
Ground tracking confirmed the orbit matches predictions for the quasi-Zenith constellation.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:54.360
The successful insertion validates the H3's performance for future high-orbit missions.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:01.060
Catalog data now lists both MichiBiki 7 and the upper stage in the planned geotransfer path.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:14.900
Yesterday's launch is therefore confirmed as fully successful. Mike Finke is leaving the agency after four space flights, 549 days in orbit, and 9 spacewalk supporting the International Space Station.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:24.960
His career included long duration expeditions and multiple shuttle missions. NASA noted that few individuals have shaped as many chapters of the agency's history.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:30.760
Finke's supported construction and maintenance of the orbiting laboratory across multiple increments.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.920
The departure closes a 30-year chapter of direct flight and operational experience.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:42.120
New data from the Einstein probe showed the quasar flaring brightly in X-rays in 2024.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:49.860
Combined with more than 20 years of archival observations, the flare indicates the object can produce normal X-ray levels.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:59.940
The study, published in the Astrophysical Journal on July 28, removes PhL-1811 from the prototype class of X-ray-week quasars.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:06.480
For more than two decades, the source had been viewed as the defining example of intrinsically faint X-ray emission.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.800
The flare observation overturned that long-standing classification.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.400
The system pairs a close-in brown dwarf with a star that has already left the main sequence.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.880
The discovery highlights how such low-mass companions can survive the expansion phases of their host stars.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:28.660
Further radial velocity monitoring will refine the brown dwarf's mass and orbital purited.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:33.960
The pairing is described as an unlikely couple given the evolutionary stage of the primary.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:39.600
Additional observations aim to pin down the exact separation and companion properties.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:49.460
The object provides evidence that rapidly growing black holes wrapped in dense gas can explain the mysterious little red dots seen in early JWST fields.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:54.980
Located at Cosmic Dawn, it shows the same spectral features as newer examples.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:01.540
The find strengthens the case that these structures formed within the first few hundred million years after the Big Bang.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:08.360
Researchers at the Institute of Science and Technology Austria and collaborators published the result in nature.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:15.280
The detection supplies the strongest evidence yet linking black hole stars to the puzzling yearly universe population.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.940
New images reveal clumpy material launched by the central star before it became a white dwarf.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:25.680
The clumps are now breaking apart and mixing with the surrounding interstellar medium.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:31.180
The observations trace how planetary nebulae enriched the galaxy with processed gas.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:36.240
The star at the heart of the helix powered a clumpy wind prior to its final collapse.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:40.800
Fresh imagery captures the ongoing dissolution of those ejected knots.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:46.940
With no moonlight to interfere, observers can expect a strong display of perseid meteors this year.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:51.300
The shower's radiant lies in perseus and remains visible through the night.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:55.520
Peak rates are forecast for the early morning hours of August 13.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:00.760
A new moon coincides with the maximum, removing the usual bright sky interference.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:05.320
Skywatchers anticipate favorable conditions across the northern hemisphere.

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:11.580
The object does not match known categories of stars, galaxies, or transients in current surveys.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:16.320
Its spectrum and variability suggest physical processes not previously catalogued.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:21.500
Follow-up observations are underway to determine its distance and energy source.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.720
The discovery was announced through community channels after initial survey data were examined.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:30.720
No existing classification accommodates the observed properties.

